\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The canonical experiment grid, instantiated identically on every benchmark. All cells use $n=64$ labeled replay examples per task and $S=5$ refinement sweeps, except $^{\dagger }$ ($S=30$; the from-pretrained cells are replicated only at the longer horizon). Entries are the mean aggregate with worst-task in parentheses---normalized retention, except twenty-task CLIP (absolute top-1 accuracy). The pretrained \emph  {alone} row is the retention zero point (CLIP-20: the $0.550$ zero-shot floor). No initialization is privileged; the two unconstrained baselines ($+$GD, $+$ungated) are run from the pretrained model and from task arithmetic, which suffices to separate the anchor and the gate from the initialization, while $+$APR is run from every initialization to test composition. The ungated update from the remaining five initializations is reported for CLIP-20 in Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:clip20matrix}. $\pm $ marks five-seed cells; the rest are single-seed. Dashes are experiments not yet run.\relax }}{6}{table.caption.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:main}{{1}{6}{The canonical experiment grid, instantiated identically on every benchmark. All cells use $n=64$ labeled replay examples per task and $S=5$ refinement sweeps, except $^{\dagger }$ ($S=30$; the from-pretrained cells are replicated only at the longer horizon). Entries are the mean aggregate with worst-task in parentheses---normalized retention, except twenty-task CLIP (absolute top-1 accuracy). The pretrained \emph {alone} row is the retention zero point (CLIP-20: the $0.550$ zero-shot floor). No initialization is privileged; the two unconstrained baselines ($+$GD, $+$ungated) are run from the pretrained model and from task arithmetic, which suffices to separate the anchor and the gate from the initialization, while $+$APR is run from every initialization to test composition. The ungated update from the remaining five initializations is reported for CLIP-20 in Table~\ref {tab:clip20matrix}. $\pm $ marks five-seed cells; the rest are single-seed. Dashes are experiments not yet run.\relax }{table.caption.8}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The same grid under split selection at a $16{+}16$ budget.} Each task contributes $16$ labeled replay examples and a \emph  {disjoint} $16$-example selection buffer ($32$ labels per task in total). Every hyperparameter---merge coefficient, density, refinement learning rate, and the sweep count $S$---is chosen by minimizing held-out loss on the selection buffer, and the evaluation split is read \emph  {only} at the selected cell. Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:main} instead tunes on the evaluation split, so the two tables answer different questions and their numbers should not be compared cell by cell: this one reports what a practitioner obtains without ever touching test data. Entries are mean aggregate with worst-task in parentheses---normalized retention, except twenty-task CLIP (absolute top-1); the pretrained \emph  {alone} row is the retention zero point. The search budget is identical across benchmarks and rows: $S\in \{5,20,50\}$ throughout. AdaMerging is given the matched budget---the same $16$ unlabeled inputs per task that APR sees labeled---rather than the standard transductive formulation that adapts on the evaluation split. $^{\dagger }$: the selected $S$ is the longest offered, so the cell is a lower bound rather than a verified interior optimum; the extended search reported in the text resolves these for the from-pretrained rows of GLUE-8 and CLIP-8. Dashes are experiments not yet run, except AdaMerging on the shared-output track, where the entropy objective requires a classification head and the baseline does not apply.\relax }}{7}{table.caption.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:main16}{{2}{7}{\textbf {The same grid under split selection at a $16{+}16$ budget.} Each task contributes $16$ labeled replay examples and a \emph {disjoint} $16$-example selection buffer ($32$ labels per task in total). Every hyperparameter---merge coefficient, density, refinement learning rate, and the sweep count $S$---is chosen by minimizing held-out loss on the selection buffer, and the evaluation split is read \emph {only} at the selected cell. Table~\ref {tab:main} instead tunes on the evaluation split, so the two tables answer different questions and their numbers should not be compared cell by cell: this one reports what a practitioner obtains without ever touching test data. Entries are mean aggregate with worst-task in parentheses---normalized retention, except twenty-task CLIP (absolute top-1); the pretrained \emph {alone} row is the retention zero point. The search budget is identical across benchmarks and rows: $S\in \{5,20,50\}$ throughout. AdaMerging is given the matched budget---the same $16$ unlabeled inputs per task that APR sees labeled---rather than the standard transductive formulation that adapts on the evaluation split. $^{\dagger }$: the selected $S$ is the longest offered, so the cell is a lower bound rather than a verified interior optimum; the extended search reported in the text resolves these for the from-pretrained rows of GLUE-8 and CLIP-8. Dashes are experiments not yet run, except AdaMerging on the shared-output track, where the entropy objective requires a classification head and the baseline does not apply.\relax }{table.caption.9}{}}
